  {
    "episode_id": 92294,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92294.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "All right. Thank you, Eric, and appreciate the chance to be with you again today for this next installment of the Virtual Legislative briefing series, here we go, getting video going. All right. Wow, what a hectic time of year. I'm guessing that if you are tuning in today, it is too wet to plant in your neck of the woods. Otherwise, it's a great time on the farm right now. I know in the next couple of weeks, we'll be getting up calves to ween and working cattle. And like all of you, there's just a lot going on as we enter this great new season. Also want to say your organization. There's a lot going on as well. I threw on the tie today to spend a little time in the cattle in the capital with our Missouri Cattleman's Association colleague Mike Deering. We were there working on one of our joint priority issues and that's in the domain reform. You all are well aware that we're in the final four weeks of the state legislative session. So there's a lot going on. If you tuned in last week, you had the chance to hear from our Senate and House. Agriculture Committee Chairman, Senator Burns Sketter, and Representative Don Rohn last week. So if you're two weeks ago, so if you missed that, make sure to go back to our Facebook page and you can pull up the video from that session. So just know that your team is hard at work every day trying to make as much progress as we can as we look at this home stretch in the state legislative session. I also want to give you all a huge thank you for making our first and hopefully maybe our only sole virtual youth leadership day a success. We were so fortunate to have Governor Parson and Director of Agriculture Chris Chen. provide commentary for that endeavor as we put together content for you to share with youth across the state. I have certainly enjoyed seeing all of the social media traffic, the creativity that you as county leaders. showed in terms of interacting with your local ag teachers and students. I've seen several instances where many ag tours were set up so the students would have the chance to visit with agribusiness leaders in addition to watching the youth leadership day video. So just a huge shout out that Couldn't be more proud of you as county leaders the way you continue to adapt and as we find new ways to continue to spread the Farm Bureau message. And in this case, help our youth understand the story of agriculture and try to help develop them. So just a great endeavor and thank Jennifer Poindexter and our public affairs team here for spearheading that effort and getting it off the ground. Also, in recent weeks, we've had lots of activity in the form of coffee with Congress. We've had the chance, and maybe some of you tuning in today have had the chance to visit with your member of Congress in one of those events that have been done both in person as well as virtually. Those have been a great opportunity to talk about what's happening in DC, in some cases, what's not happening. We've also had capital connection in the state capital. And this past week, a couple of leaders from northeast Missouri were here, Miss Andy Jackson and Caleb, the collegiate president at Curbsville at Truman State. So we certainly appreciate it when us county leaders are willing to raise your hand and Make a trip to the state capital to talk about Missouri Farm Bureau's priority issues with your legislators and others that are serving here in the state legislature. So those are a few things going on. I will add that Kelly Smith provided an update a few weeks ago on the Black Vulture Deportation Program that Missouri Farm Bureau is partnering with the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service on. Kelly has been busy fielding calls and providing information and applications to landowners who are facing issues with black mulcher. So again, he continues and will continue to be the point of contact for Missouri Farm Bureau. So if you have any questions, please feel free to reach out to Kelly at 573-893-1416. Again, 573-893-1416. And finally, before I pitch it to Spencer for a deep dive into infrastructure, since that is the topic du jour in DC. I want to note that, again, there's a lot going on, tax policy and the intersection of that with infrastructure. Just a lot to pay attention to. Make sure also that you take a look at the new issue of the Show Me Missouri Farm Bureau magazine that's hitting mailboxes now. There are updates on feral hogs, a great piece on agritourism, ventures in Missouri. So make sure to check it out. So Spencer, I'll turn it to you.",
