  {
    "episode_id": 92294,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92294.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Garrett, it's great to be with you. It's good to see you as president of Farm Bureau. I'm glad to have you in that capacity and see where you've been. What's going on up here is is truly tragic, serving on the Ways and Means Committee and helping be one of the co-authors of the 2017 Tax Cut and Jobs Act. We were able to do some pretty significant changes to the tax code that helped farmers and agriculture businesses and just all families throughout the country, really huge impacts in southern Missouri and outstate Missouri. But something that And what's being proposed right now is just virtually reversing most of that. The upcoming proposal of just increasing the business rate from 21 to 28% will have a huge impact, not just on those businesses, but on their employees, their wages. They will ultimately be the ones that's getting the the effect of it more than anyone. And that's a domino effect that's pretty tragic. If you're looking at the proposals of what's out there from 21 to 28% on the business rate, that will actually be higher than the business rate for communist China. And that right there creates a competitive disadvantage where virtually a factory in Mexico, Missouri would be rewarded by firing their employees, closing their factory in Mexico and moving to Beijing. That's what this proposal ultimately does. Something that we've also worked on to try to completely eliminate is the death tax. The death tax or the estate tax, however, it's the death tax. We initially was very successful in the 2017 tax cuts. The house pass version completely eliminating the estate tax, the death tax. But then through the negotiations, through the reconciliation issues, through the bird rolls, and they didn't completely eliminate it, but we doubled it. We doubled it to roughly 11 million per person, a couple 22 million. But that only lasts until 2025, and then it expires and goes back to half of that, five and a half million per person. However, the proposal that's coming forward in these tranches of packages that the administration is offering will bring it down to three and a half million per person. That's a huge change from 11 to 3.5 million. And people are going to say, this is for the wealthy. Let me tell you, a farmer in southeast Missouri in our congressional district where you're talking about per acre is eight to $10,000 per acre. It doesn't take much whenever a loved one passes on. And that family farm that's been there a long time to be over these thresholds. Then they have the share to the Uncle Sam And it's just, it's a horrible domino effect. But what's also even worse is what they're proposing is eliminating the step up basis. That right there is a second death tax, in my opinion. And it will be devastating, devastating to the folks across rural America and even in urban areas where small businesses are or whatever you have it, and then that increase the capital gains tax. That's another effect that you'll see throughout this. We've got a lot of fights on our hands, and I'll tell you, we're the sponsor of the original death tax repeal bill, and it's filed right now. We have 140 sponsors. That's the most we've ever had. in Congress, even whenever Republicans were in the majority, we did not have this many as co-sponsors. So we're building some support. I will say it's bipartisan. We only have one Democrat, but we're trying for more. When you're in a majority right now in Congress, Speaker Pelosi, if we can pick up three Democrats, Gary, if we can pick up three Democrats, we can stop this stuff. And that's what we have to work in trying to make sure that these policies don't come forward. But we got our hands full. There's no question. And whether it's in the Ways and Means Committee or the Budget Committee that I serve on, this is what we're doing the full court press on. So if these policies come forward, you're talking about how devastating from a tax perspective, and I haven't even started on some of the environmental Green New Deal items that's proposed in these packages, but we've got a lot before us. So I'd be happy to take questions or whatever you all want, but that's kind of a summary of the current tax proposal and the one that's coming in tranches.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
