  {
    "episode_id": 92294,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92294.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "OK. So you mentioned Green New Deal a few minutes ago. And I think we're all very aware at this point that all things carbon seems to be spanning. The president was serious when he talked about an all the government approach to climate change. Can you at least, you've been part of this discussion for a number of years now, tell me how much time is actually spent on the science aspect anymore in terms of what we're going to achieve in terms of the outcome versus the prescriptions? Because one of the questions that have come in is just, okay, what are we hoping for in terms of the outcome with all that's being put on the table here? So how would you frame up the discussions as you hear them on Capitol Hill with all things climate.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Green New Deal has been discussed recently.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "There is widespread awareness that carbon-related issues are central to current discourse.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The president emphasized a comprehensive government-led approach to climate change.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Climate policy discussions on Capitol Hill focus heavily on prescriptions rather than scientific outcomes.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "One key question raised by participants is the desired outcome of proposed climate measures.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that scientific evidence should play a more prominent role in climate policy debates.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener has sufficient expertise and experience to evaluate the balance between science and policy prescriptions.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "A clear articulation of intended outcomes is essential for credible climate policy design.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Policy discussions on Capitol Hill prioritize political feasibility over scientific rigor.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The public expects transparency about both the means and ends of climate initiatives.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92294,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92294.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Jared, facts and science are words that the other side of the aisle likes to use a lot, but not always pay attention to. If they don't like the facts or the science, then it doesn't matter. We can say we've seen that with the We can, I mean, children under two definitely don't need a mask, but that's not what you always get here. But that's a different tangent. When you talk about climate and these policies, Look no farther than our farmers, Gary. And this is what we have to do a better job about is showing how we are the best stewards of our land, that we are the true conservationists, that in the Republican party is a party of conservation. We have always been, but Republicans sometimes are afraid to embrace and to talk about it. And whether you look at the soil and water programs that we do in the state of Missouri. They are so helpful and that really take care of our land, but also allows us to be more efficient and utilize it. That's what we need to do. Whether it's the trillion trees program that President Trump worked with Leader McCarthy and also Congressman Westerman from Arkansas. These are ways to to help with climate and those issues by planting more trees. This is something we do in Missouri. We believe in using our forest. I mean, we're huge promoters of the Mark Twain and making sure that our forest is properly managed and it's a renewable resource. harvest those trees that are dying, plant the new ones, and it creates an economic engine for all of the industries that we see throughout our state, multiplying effect. But there's climate approaches that can be done in a fashion that every conservative and every liberal would be able to embrace. Pretty, it just makes sense. However, a lot of people are just wanting to throw the baby out with the bath water and trying to push policies that are unrealistic and not approached. We have to use all of our resources in this country to think that everyone's going to have an electric car in southeast Missouri or throughout our state. That's not going to happen. We have folks that that don't even have running water in some parts of our state, and they're just trying to put food on their table. But they probably have a much smaller carbon footprint than Zark Kerry, who flies in his private jet all over the country. We can work together, but the policies that they are pushing right now and the infrastructure package aren't the policies that work. The $2.3 trillion infrastructure proposal that's coming in, $600 billion is funding the Green New Deal initiative. The word Green New Deal? is not popular, so now they're trying to call it infrastructure. And that's what they're doing. I mean, you look at this infrastructure package. For example, it's coming through Congressman Graves' Committee and also through the Budget Committee in the end, where the ones who brings ways and means and transportation and E&C altogether Plus, 6% is going to roads and bridges. Less than 2% is going to locks and dams and airports and ports, which is extremely important. We all want those provisions. Less than 5% is going to broadband. Those are items that Republicans and Democrats will tell you that is infrastructure, where we get into the other issue. Now they're coming up with a new definition of infrastructure, human infrastructure, environmental infrastructure. Let's expand Medicaid infrastructure. It's something. But we could work on some things, and I hope they choose to go that path. We're waving willing. I know Congressman Graves has met with the president. I know that some other members of transportation committee has met with the president in the last week. He says he wants to work together, but it hasn't showed it yet.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
