  {
    "episode_id": 92294,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92294.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "I think you're going to start seeing it get pretty busy in May. Today is April 15th. April 15th is the deadline for Congress to pass a budget. Infrastructure is going to be pushed through reconciliation, which is created by the passage of a budget. As of last week, The President Biden. He created history, in fact, a Garrett. He was the latest president in the history of this country to offer a budget, and it wasn't even really a budget. It didn't have anything in it in regards to mandatory spending, which makes up 75% of the budget. It was only 58 pages. They call it a skinny budget. I would say it's a back of an envelope budget. In fact, in that budget that was presented last week, which was more than 70 days late, Climate change was mentioned 146 times. Border security was mentioned three. And clearly, we have a huge crisis at our southern border. And we've had a 71% increase of illegal migrants crossing the border just in the last 60 days. But Homeland Security had a 0% increase in funding in that proposal. But everything else had an average of 16% across the board increase. They got a long way to go before they get this budget passed. And they have to pass the budget to get the reconciliation in order to move the infrastructure package. What I would say, Garrett, is that they may try to pass pass an infrastructure package, not going through reconciliation out of the house. By the time the Senate gets it, then they'll be through the process of maybe doing the budget, budget resolution with the reconciliation to move forward. I do know I met with Senator Graham, who is my counterpart on the Senate Budget Committee. Bernie Sanders is also my counterpart. Well, we don't have a lot in common. There is an attempt for a bipartisan infrastructure package, but I don't think that the squad on this side of the building is going to go for that by the time it gets there. But the bipartisan transportation package that's being negotiated right now in the Senate is roughly $600 to $700 billion in its truly infrastructure. I think you could see a lot of Republicans get behind it and it would be funded an appropriate way instead of like on the backs of farmers and working class Missourians. But I would say mid May, you're going to see an aggressive, aggressive push. But July is probably when it's going to, it's really going to get serious.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
